1. INTRODUCTION

A restaurant recommendation system can help users discover restaurants that match their preferences. Recommendation systems can use restaurant characteristics such as cuisine, location, pricing, ratings, and available services to identify suitable options.

As part of the Cognifyz Technologies Machine Learning Internship, this task focuses on developing a content-based restaurant recommendation system. The system accepts user preferences and recommends restaurants by first applying explicit preference-based filters and then ranking the resulting restaurants according to content similarity and restaurant rating.

2. OBJECTIVE

The main objectives of this task are:

• To develop a restaurant recommendation system based on user preferences.
• To preprocess restaurant information required for recommendation.
• To define recommendation criteria such as city, cuisine, price range, minimum rating, and online delivery.
• To implement a content-based filtering approach.
• To use TF-IDF to represent textual restaurant information.
• To calculate similarity using cosine similarity.
• To combine similarity and restaurant rating for ranking.
• To return the Top N restaurants matching the user's requirements.
• To test the recommendation system using different preference combinations.
• To handle invalid inputs and cases where no restaurants match the selected criteria.

3. DATASET DESCRIPTION

The restaurant dataset provided for the internship contains 9,551 restaurant records and 21 attributes.

The dataset contains information including Restaurant ID, Restaurant Name, Country Code, City, Address, Locality, Longitude, Latitude, Cuisines, Average Cost for two, Currency, Has Table booking, Has Online delivery, Is delivering now, Switch to order menu, Price range, Aggregate rating, Rating color, Rating text, and Votes.

For the recommendation task, restaurant attributes related to city, locality, cuisine, pricing, ratings, and services were used.

4. DATA PREPARATION

A separate recommendation dataset was prepared from the cleaned restaurant dataset.

The recommendation system uses information including:

• Restaurant Name
• City
• Locality
• Cuisines
• Price range
• Aggregate rating
• Has Online delivery
• Has Table booking

Text-based fields were cleaned by handling missing values, converting values to strings, removing unnecessary spaces, and normalizing text.

The recommendation process does not require the restaurant rating to be non-zero because a restaurant can still be a valid candidate when rating is not used as a strict requirement.

5. RECOMMENDATION FEATURES

The recommendation system uses the following information:

• City
• Cuisine
• Locality
• Price range
• Aggregate rating
• Online delivery availability
• Table booking availability

These attributes provide information that can be used to identify restaurants matching a user's requirements.

6. RECOMMENDATION CRITERIA

The recommendation system accepts the following user inputs:

• City
• Cuisine
• Price range
• Minimum rating
• Online delivery
• Number of recommendations

These parameters allow users to specify both their preferred restaurant characteristics and filtering constraints.

7. CONTENT-BASED FILTERING

A content-based filtering approach was implemented for restaurant recommendation.

Unlike collaborative filtering, which requires historical interactions between users and items, the implemented system uses the characteristics of the restaurants themselves.

The recommendation workflow is:

User Preferences
        ↓
Input Validation
        ↓
Preference-Based Filtering
        ↓
Matching Restaurants
        ↓
TF-IDF Representation
        ↓
Cosine Similarity
        ↓
Rating Score
        ↓
Final Ranking Score
        ↓
Top N Recommendations

8. CONTENT REPRESENTATION

Restaurant textual information was transformed into a representation suitable for similarity calculation.

Cuisine and location-related information were used to represent restaurant content for the TF-IDF-based similarity calculation.

The resulting textual representation allows the system to compare the user's requested cuisine and city with restaurant information and calculate a similarity score.

9. TF-IDF VECTORIZATION

TF-IDF, or Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency, was used to convert textual information into numerical vectors.

The TF-IDF method assigns greater importance to terms that are informative within the restaurant dataset while reducing the relative importance of terms that occur frequently across many records.

A TF-IDF representation was created for the restaurant content, and the user's cuisine and city preferences were also transformed into the same vector space.

10. COSINE SIMILARITY

Cosine similarity was used to measure the similarity between the user preference vector and the restaurant content vectors.

The similarity score ranges according to the degree of alignment between the represented texts.

A higher similarity score indicates that the restaurant content is more closely aligned with the user's specified cuisine and location preferences.

The similarity score is used as the primary component of the recommendation ranking.

11. USER PREFERENCE FILTERING

Before calculating similarity, restaurants are filtered using the explicit preferences supplied by the user.

The filtering criteria include:

• City
• Cuisine
• Price range
• Minimum rating
• Online delivery preference

This ensures that the recommendation results satisfy the user's specified constraints before the similarity-based ranking is applied.

12. RECOMMENDATION FUNCTION

A reusable function named recommend_restaurants() was implemented to generate recommendations.

The function accepts the following parameters:

• city
• cuisine
• price_range
• min_rating
• online_delivery
• top_n

The function performs the following operations:

1. Validates user inputs.
2. Creates a copy of the recommendation dataset.
3. Normalizes text fields.
4. Filters restaurants according to user preferences.
5. Creates the user preference representation.
6. Calculates TF-IDF-based similarity.
7. Calculates a normalized rating score.
8. Calculates a final ranking score.
9. Sorts restaurants by the final score.
10. Returns the requested number of recommendations.

Input validation was included to handle invalid values such as an empty city, empty cuisine, invalid price range, or minimum rating outside the allowed range.

13. RATING SCORE

In addition to similarity, restaurant rating was incorporated into the ranking process.

The rating was normalized to a 0-1 scale using:

Rating Score = Aggregate Rating / 5

This normalization makes the restaurant rating numerically comparable with the similarity score.

14. FINAL RANKING SCORE

The final ranking score was calculated as:

Final Score = 0.7 × Similarity Score + 0.3 × Rating Score

The larger weight assigned to similarity ensures that the recommendations remain primarily aligned with the user's selected cuisine and location preferences.

The rating score acts as a secondary quality-related factor.

The weights of 0.7 and 0.3 are design choices used in this implementation and are not externally learned from user feedback.

Restaurants are sorted in descending order of Final Score, and the Top N restaurants are returned.

15. SAMPLE USER PREFERENCE

The recommendation system was tested using sample user preferences.

Example:

City: New Delhi
Cuisine: North Indian
Price Range: 2
Minimum Rating: 4.0
Online Delivery: Yes
Number of Recommendations: 10

The function filters restaurants according to these requirements and returns the highest-ranked matching restaurants.

